```json
[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/cli/api-token.ts",
  "line": 35,
  "title": "Missing-record fallback ignores the configured daemon port",
  "detail": "Without an `api-token.origin` record (an older daemon), `apiTokenHeaders` authorizes only `http://127.0.0.1:${LIMITLESS_PORT ?? 7400}`. With `[server] port = 7412` in config.toml and no `LIMITLESS_PORT`, setup targets 7412 correctly but sends no authorization header, so authenticated calls to an older daemon fail. The helper would also send the token to port 7400 when that isn't the daemon's port. Measured with a temporary home and a synthetic token. Fix: resolve the effective daemon port the same way the CLI and setup do (the configured `[server] port`, overridden by `LIMITLESS_PORT`). Use one implementation, have every caller pass it, and use it for the fallback origin. Test: no record, configured port 7412, no env \u2192 the 7412 origin gets the header, 7400 doesn't."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/cli/api-token.ts",
  "line": 39,
  "title": "After a rollback, newer clients lose access until the stale origin record is removed",
  "detail": "An older daemon started after a rollback rotates `api-token` but leaves the newer release's `api-token.origin` untouched. On the same listener origin with enforcement enabled, the previous release's CLI got HTTP 200 and this PR's CLI got 401, because the record's `tokenHash` no longer matches. AGENTS.md requires the previous release to keep working across a rollback, and a newer CLI talking to an older daemon is part of that. Fix: treat a record whose hash doesn't match the current token as absent, and fall back to the configured loopback origin (the previous finding). That is exactly the trust an older daemon without a record gets: the record can only narrow the recipient, never widen it. Keep rejecting a record whose origin isn't a plain http(s) origin. Test: a record with a mismatched hash and a token on the configured loopback port \u2192 the header goes to that port only."
 }
]
